From 3deca7f78f54af133f520637eac2857e1e700b3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:26:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(attachables): render equipped native pack models

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) sends Hive angel wings as chest
attachables, separate from player skins. Resolve canonical converted item
identities and submit their native geometry through a player equipment
layer instead of expecting the skin renderer to contain worn meshes.

Retain playback per player, slot and item so spawn animations finish.
Apply ordered bone visibility, third-person Molang context, inherited
alpha-test materials, authored UVs and same-name owner bone deformation against complete global bind matrices.
Retry parent setup when existing owner graph scopes load or change, and
clear connection-owned resources on reload. Suppress duplicate vanilla
head items only after a supported native head surface is selected.

Native evidence includes Hive chest item 10173 angel_wings_pink, head
item 10209 axe, six zero-thickness south-facing wing planes, inherited
DisableCulling and a held spawn scale reaching one after 0.64 seconds.
Keep those planes unchanged. Explicit binding expressions, non-default
variants and unverified material families retain the existing fallback.
Classic owners without a native graph/controller lack a parent variable
scope; this does not prevent independent equipped wing playback.

Targeted tests cover identity collisions, equipment lifetime, held spawn
playback, owner context, ordered visibility and animated body binding, including rotated native ancestor rest poses.

Preserve full same-name owner deformation matrices for equipped models. Rotated bones under nonuniform or reflected scale require shear, and independently bound children can have a singular parent. Avoid parent inversion and TRS decomposition. Keep local animation poses intact, inherit full matrices for unmatched descendants, and clear bindings on each pose or replacement binding pass. Existing full-matrix drawing and bounds scopes also apply to bound models. Targeted tests fail against the previous binding and pass for shear, reflections, singular parents, entity-relative descendants, locators, drawing normals, and repeated binding. This preserves the supported binding rule; explicit native binding expressions and complete first-person equipment parity remain separate gaps.

Resolve equipped definitions, materials, animation graphs, and textures
from core's accepted native archive on direct and ViaProxy connections.
Use the same parsed storage and pack priority on both routes; Java equipment
identities remain the input to the existing per-slot playback cache. Missing
assets retain fallback without forcing a Store session. Existing limits on
explicit bindings, material families, and variants still need implementation.

Select explicit item bindings from the native item identity and the current
wearer, properties, and equipment context. This supports definitions such
as iron_chestplate.player without confusing their attachable identifier
with the inventory item identifier. Preserve each slot's playback until
the selected definition changes. Bounded expression caches and per-avatar
query state prevent cross-owner state leakage. Pack and world lifetimes
release selector state with models and textures. Conditions and resource
metadata round trips have targeted regressions; native comparisons retain
separate gaps for bindings, material families, and first-person equipment.

Replace the corresponding vanilla armor surface only after supported native
geometry, texture, and material selection succeeds. The target native
iron_chestplate.player override renders wings without vanilla iron armor.
Clearing only the vanilla head item left duplicate chest, leg, and foot
armor visible in Java. Unsupported native surfaces retain vanilla fallback.

Match costume visibility against the same humanoid name scope as actor
visibility. Legacy player skin imports fold bone names to ASCII lowercase,
while server controller rules retain their authored spelling. Cached
patterns restore the arm and sleeve without changing exact matching for
custom actors or the order of explicit visibility overrides.

Load the licensed 1.26.51.1 equipment library below accepted server packs.
Resolve declared legacy equipment geometry through the existing native
inheritance rules, including parents in another file or a higher pack.
Native player armor definitions need these parents and owner root-locator
queries; falling back to Java armor discards custom body offsets.

Tests cover native owner bindings, replacement conditions and cross-file
inheritance with an accepted parent override. The bundled iron chestplate
resolves its geometry, alpha-test material and native animation in a private
resource probe. Full visible armor parity still needs the reported skin.

Prepare native equipment asynchronously and retain ordinary equipment until its storage is ready. Collect the geometries and textures referenced by attachables, resolve legacy parents against the complete inherited library, then omit unrelated actor models and UI assets from equipment storage. This preserves cross-file inheritance and server override order while avoiding a render-thread parse of the whole licensed library. On the saved CubeCraft pack, equipment storage retains 24 relevant geometries instead of 1,005.

Render supported held attachables in both views without server overrides.
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 shield and spyglass geometry binds to the equipped item
bone, and the native wield/scoping controllers distinguish main_hand,
off_hand and first-person context. Evaluate binding expressions, retain
separate view clocks and the visible first-person item stacks, and combine
the owner bone matrix with the attachable origin. The Creator attachables
reference documents rightItem/leftItem slot binding:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/attachables

Suppress a Java held draw only after every native surface resolves. Keep
Java rendering for glint, banner atlases, unsupported materials, or missing
owner bones. The Java-hand preference bypasses native held drawing too.

Apply the current equipment's parent_setup after every owner pre_animation
reset and before its animation graph. The target player script resets
armor-layer visibility each frame, so one-time setup cannot preserve the
authored hides. Removing equipment also removes its setup from the next
frame. Compile each item and slot setup once; keep GUI selectors and
first-person equipment lifetimes separate. A numerical regression covers
repeated owner resets and unequip, and held bindings validate the actual
selected first-person owner before suppressing a Java draw.

Reproduce target Bedrock texture_mesh voxel geometry for equipped sprites,
including the native crossbow. Resolve the selected render-controller texture
from the accepted pack stack, retain geometry and item-slot bone bindings, and
cache each geometry/texture result independently. This prevents a parsed but
empty native sprite geometry from suppressing Java rendering.

Evidence: Minecraft Windows 1.26.51.1, PE SHA-256
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.
Parser 0x1460fdbf0 initializes position, local_pivot, and rotation to zero,
scale to [1,1,1], and use_pixel_depth to true. Bone construction at
0x141c1b620 retains each sprite's authored transform. Rendering at
0x141c22b70 divides authored texture dimensions by the actual image dimensions
and scales the extrusion depth by the larger dimension ratio (xmm11 at
0x141c23136, multiplied by scale.y at 0x141c2316c). Translation
helper 0x1459cba80 and matrix multiply 0x140544f00 establish the order:
scale, subtract unscaled local_pivot, rotate X/Y/Z, add position. The sprite
builder at 0x145b27ff0 emits texel voxels in X/Z, accepts alpha >=2, and uses
max(image width,height)/16 when use_pixel_depth is true. Native crossbow
geometry/controllers select standby/pulling0/pulling1/pulling2/arrow/rocket
frames, with charged Java projectile components mapped to native frames 4/5.
Require every sprite texture alias to resolve to the selected controller
texture; multi-texture geometry retains Java until separate materials are supported.
The official geometry schema describes texture_meshes as texel-to-voxel meshes:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/visualreference/geometry.v1.21.0

Bound dimensions to 256 and total voxels to 8192; unsupported sprites retain
Java rendering. Maps and active crossbow charging still use a consistent Java
special-renderer fallback until native map controls and charging frame timing
are supported. Do not claim complete charging or map-rendering parity.
